- The members of a top-level `impl` block or trait (functions, constants, associated types) are listed one level deeper, each with its own line range from its leading annotations, so an agent can read one method of a long `impl` block.
- An inline module's items are listed one level deeper too, by the same rules as top-level ones (its private `use` isn't listed); an `impl` block inside a module lists its members at depth 2.
- Nothing below depth 2 is listed: the items of `deeper`, a module inside `inner`, are at depth 2, so the `impl` block among them prints without its member.
- Macro calls inside an `impl` block aren't members.
== args
input/shapes.rs
== file input/shapes.rs
//! Shapes.

pub trait Shape {
    type Unit: Copy;
    const SIDES: u8;
    /// The area.
    fn area(&self) -> f64;
}

impl Square {
    pub const ONE: Self = Self(1);

    /// Makes a square.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn new(side: u32) -> Self {
        Self(side)
    }

    delegate!(side);
}

mod inner {
    use super::Square;

    impl Square {
        fn side(&self) -> u32 { self.0 }
    }

    mod deeper {
        fn hidden() {}

        impl super::Square {
            fn hidden_too() {}
        }
    }
}
